#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Let Claude Code and Codex sessions on this host message each other by name.

Claude sessions already message each other natively (SendMessage, /list-agents).
Neither tool lets an outside program speak that protocol: its wire format is
internal, and Channels, the documented way in, is blocked by the org policy.
So each Codex session gets a stand-in: a headless Claude session named after it
(`claude -p --input-format stream-json --name <title>`). Being a real Claude
session, it is listed by /list-agents, takes SendMessage, and sends with it. Two
documented extension points connect it to the Codex side:

  in   a UserPromptSubmit hook hands each arriving message to the daemon, which
       queues it into the Codex session (`codex queue`), and blocks the turn, so
       the stand-in's model never runs for it;
  out  the daemon writes a trigger line to the stand-in's stdin; its model calls
       SendMessage with placeholders, a PreToolUse hook replaces the whole input
       with the real text, and a PostToolUse hook ends the turn at once - so the
       model never sees the text and a message arriving meanwhile starts a turn
       of its own, through the hook, instead of being read in passing.

Codex sessions send with `agent-bridge send` (run from their shell, which
carries CODEX_THREAD_ID); a message between two Codex sessions goes straight to
`codex queue`. The daemon starts on demand (any subcommand but status and stop) and
keeps one stand-in per live Codex session that has had a first message.
"""
